# Tests for the accounting of unfolding steps (the -steps budget)

# A user wrapper around a class method (x + 1 on Integer or Bit#(8))
# costs no step: the wrapper is harmless and the method it selects
# is simple, so a chain of 300 wrappers fits in a budget of 100 steps
compile_verilog_pass HarmlessChain.bsv sysHarmlessChain "-steps 100"

# A class method that recurses through its own dictionary is counted
# at every selection of the method, so the recursion runs out of
# steps (G0024) instead of running unbounded
compile_verilog_fail_error RecursiveMethod.bsv G0024 1 "" "-steps 100"

# The same recursion through a user wrapper around the method: the
# wrapper is not harmless, since the method it selects reaches the
# wrapper again
compile_verilog_fail_error RecursiveMethodWrapper.bsv G0024 1 "" "-steps 100"

# A recursive function called through a local alias of itself (bound,
# since it is used twice): the alias is the reference to the function,
# so every call through it is an unfolding that counts, and the chain
# of 30 fits in a budget of 400 steps
compile_verilog_pass RecursiveAlias.bsv sysRecursiveAlias "-steps 400"

# The alias bound outside the function's lambda, so that it holds the
# definition's value: the recursion still runs out of steps (G0024)
compile_verilog_fail_error RecursiveAliasLet.bs G0024 1 "" "-steps 100"

# The same without a base case: the steps budget must stop it (the heap
# cap turns an uncounted recursion into a failure rather than a hang)
compile_verilog_fail_error RecursiveAliasInf.bs G0024 1 "" \
    "-steps 100 +RTS -M512M -RTS"
